0229339599609375, -0.0027790069580078125, 0.0223388671875, 3.4928321838378906e-05, 0.00627899169921875, 0.01302337646484375, 0.020751953125, -0.0231170654296875, 0.020538330078125, 0.0174560546875, -0.01059722900390625, 0.0032863616943359375, -0.026824951171875, -0.009979248046875, -0.030975341796875, -0.0034236907958984375, 0.0201416015625, -0.0174102783203125, -8.308887481689453e-05, -0.0086822509765625, -0.00728607177734375, -0.00899505615234375, -0.01354217529296875, 0.0094146728515625, 0.050537109375, -0.03338623046875, -0.0179901123046875, -0.0167236328125, 0.01236724853515625, -0.00679779052734375, 0.01445770263671875, 0.0117950439453125, 0.003314971923828125, 0.0103759765625, -0.03802490234375, -0.0216522216796875, -0.023284912109375, -0.005252838134765625, 0.0377197265625, -0.03662109375, 0.020965576171875, -0.05426025390625, -0.01186370849609375, -0.018310546875, 0.003238677978515625, 0.0018596649169921875, -0.0130462646484375, -0.018463134765625, 0.0025653839111328125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "This short work presents a novel extension of the permanental process model by incorporating fixed effects, the first such generalization to our knowledge. The motivation for introducing fixed effects is to allow direct modeling of known covariates (e.g., experimental conditions or baseline trends), which enhances interpretability and improves estimation accuracy in scientific and applied settings where such information is available.\n\nWe begin by placing a prior on the coefficients of the fixed effects. In particular, we consider the \"diffuse prior limit,\" where the prior variance on these coefficients becomes arbitrarily large, reflecting minimal prior information. In this limit, the fixed effects are estimated solely from the data. Using this limiting form, we apply the representer theorem to the permanental process: specifically, we show that the intensity function naturally decomposes into a term corresponding to the fixed effects and a nonparametric function lying in a Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space (RKHS).\n\nMathematically, the representer theorem implies that the solution to the regularized estimation problem can be represented as a sum of basis functions associated with the fixed effects and kernel evaluations at the observed data points. The regularization penalty in the limiting case induces a specific equivalent kernel, known as the \"limiting equivalent kernel,\" which defines the inner product and norm of the corresponding RKHS. This RKHS structure is significant because it rigorously determines which functions are favored by the prior and guides the form of penalization in the estimation procedure.\n\nThis framework leads to more interpretable models, as the fixed effects can be directly associated with specific scientific variables (such as known experimental factors), while the RKHS component flexibly captures residual structure. As an example, prior knowledge about covariate effects can be incorporated either as fixed effects or into the choice of kernel, supporting tailored modeling for domain-specific applications. Thus, the extension enables straightforward interpretation of permanental process models and facilitates the routine inclusion of domain knowledge in estimation."}}